2026/1739 OF THE EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT AND THE COUNCIL OF THE EUROPEAN UNION,
amending Regulations (EU) No 1308/2013, (EU) 2021/2115 and (EU) 2021/2116 as regards the strengthening of the position of farmers in the food supply chain
THE EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT AND THE COUNCIL OF THE EUROPEAN UNION,
Having regard to the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union, and in particular Article 42, the first paragraph, and Article 43(2),
Having regard to the proposal from the European Commission,
After transmission of the draft legislative act to the national parliaments,
Having regard to the opinion of the European Economic and Social Committee ( 1 ) ,
Having regard to the opinion of the Committee of the Regions ( 2 ) ,
Acting in accordance with the ordinary legislative procedure ( 3 ) ,
In developing marketing standards related to the indication of the place of farming and/or place of origin, particular attention should be paid to the specific context of each product and sector. Those indications should be determined in light of the need for better compliance with transparency prospects, including through the labelling of a country of origin in accordance with the rules of the internal market, their relevance to consumers and the structure of the supply chain, taking into account the practical implications for operators and the need to avoid unnecessary complexity.
Different operators within the agricultural and food supply chain, active at different stages of production, processing, marketing, distribution and retail, developed schemes and labels to promote commercial modes ensuring a fair and added value allocation for farmers and the creation and maintenance of short supply chains, need to establish minimum requirements for the use of optional terms reporting on these commercial methods to increase the transparency and reliability of the use of those terms in the food supply chain, complementing the existing rules on food labelling, in particular the rules in Regulation (EU) No 1169/2011 of the European Parliament and of the Council ( 4 ).
In the interests of confidence and increased equity along the food supply chain, the terms ‘equivalent’, ‘equivalent’ or terms having equivalent meanings should only be used for the designation of commercial methods ensuring stability and transparency in commercial relations between farmers and buyers and price which may be considered by participating farmers to be equitable and profitable, and which support and contribute to the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals, including in a manner consistent with Annex I to Directive (EU) 2024/1760 of the European Parliament and of the Council ( 5 ).
While Member States may maintain or introduce national law setting out supplementary requirements for the use of optional terms for commercial modules, that law should not prevent or restrict the use of those terms for products lawfully produced or lawfully marketed in another Member State.
In order to reflect developments in the market and to maintain effective competition in the dairy market at the same time, it is appropriate to increase the quantitative limits applicable to the volume of raw milk covered by contractual negotiations by recognised producer organisations, expressed as a percentage of the total Union production and of the total amounts produced and delivered in a Member State.
In order to promote further sustainable development, which is the core principle of the Treaties and which is a priority objective for Union policies, and to ensure transparency, stability and equity in commercial relations between farmers and buyers across the supply chain, Member States should be able to recognise producer organisations that pursue specific objectives by using optional terms for commercial modules, such as ‘equivalent’ or ‘equitably’, or terms with equivalent senses to those terms, and the term ‘short supply chain’.
The statutes of producer organisations require their members to limit membership to one producer organisation for any particular product to create stability for the producer organisation, to prevent the transport of members by limiting their possibility to restrict their quantities to different producer organisations, and to maintain coherence with their membership representation. At the same time, a producer should be able to become a member of different producer organisations if the product concerned is a different product of the holding. It is therefore appropriate to clarify that there are numerous memberships in duly justified cases, provided that the products are quite different, having regard, inter alia, to their specific characteristics or to the intended final uses.
In order to prevent purchasers from drawing up the marketing position of producer organisations, appropriate safeguards should be established for contacts between buyers and the members of those producer organisations. While purchasers may contact members of producer organisations, those members should not be contacted on the basis of the objectives of the producer organisations, or of the concentration of supply and the placing of products on the market, in particular, where such direct contact is used with members of producer organisations to go around the common strategy of producer organisations.
In order to strengthen the position of dairy farmers, in particular through strengthening their producer organisations, it is appropriate to align the rules on the statutes of producer organisations and to extend the application of those rules to producer organisations in the dairy sector, thereby enhancing democratic functioning and transparency.
Interbranch organisations play an important role in facilitating dialogue between actors in the supply chain, and in promoting best practices, market transparency, stability and balance in commercial relations between farmers and buyers across the supply chain. It is therefore appropriate to promote initiatives to pursue optional terms for commercial methods, such as the terms ‘equivalent’, ‘equivalent’ or terms with equivalent meanings to those terms, and to include the term ‘short supply chain’ in the list of objectives that can be pursued by a recognised interbranch organisation.
In order to better take into account market signs and to improve price transmission, Member States should be able to require written contracts for delivery of agricultural products, including delivery by other operators in or to the food supply chain, and require purchasers to use written tenders for delivery of contracts for agricultural products.
The requirement to use written contracts for the delivery of agricultural products and the basic conditions for their use should be laid down at Union level, while ensuring that the parties should not be restricted to all aspects of their contracts beyond what is essential. However, Member States should not be prevented from adopting measures to combat unfair trade practices in the agricultural and food supply chain which have a negative impact on the living standards of the agricultural community, provided that those measures are appropriate and proportionate to ensure that the objective pursued is achieved and provided that they are compatible with Directive (EU) 2019/633 of the European Parliament and of the Council ( 8), including Article 9 thereof.
In order to encourage parties to find a friendly solution for disputes in relation to the conclusion of a written contract or the revision of a written contract, Member States should ensure that there is a competitive intervention mechanism or mechanisms for the parties. Such mechanisms may include existing mechanisms or new mechanisms may be established for that purpose. These may include, inter alia, organisations representing farmers, inter-branch organisations, accredited private intervention services or other independent dispute resolution bodies. Those mechanisms should be impartial and should be accessible to the contracting parties on a voluntary basis. Member States should inform the Commission of the mechanisms in place in their territory.
In order to facilitate the functioning of price transmission mechanisms, where the final price payable for the delivery of agricultural products is calculated by combining different factors set out in the contract, those factors should include objective indicators, indexes or methods of calculation which are easy for the parties to understand. In order that farmers are not legally obliged to sell below their production cost, the final price indicators, indices and calculation methods should reflect changes in market conditions and relevant aspects of the production costs of the agricultural products delivered, which influence the remuneration of farmers.
In view of the vulnerable situation of the negotiation of farmers and their organisations, recent cases involving significant volatility in agricultural input costs and market prices, and the need for more efficient price transmission in the supply chain, a review clause should be included in contracts in the dairy sector of a length of more than 6 months, and in contracts the duration of which in other agricultural sectors is more than 12 months, which may be applied by farmers or their organisations, for example in the event of unforeseen circumstances, such as weather events, outbreaks of animal diseases, geopolitic tension, or any other reason. The clause should allow farmers to request a review of the characteristics of the contract at any time after the first 6 or 12 months, as is relevant, and to allow them to end the contract where an agreement is not reached. That amount should not prejudice the other parties' rights to be negotiated for the revision of the contract.
In order to improve contractual transparency and to contribute to more balanced trading practices, Member States should be able to require written contracts for the delivery of agricultural products.
Certain vertical and horizontal cooperation initiatives relating to agricultural and food products, which aim to apply stricter requirements than the mandatory requirements, may have positive effects on the objective of the common agricultural policy to ensure a fair standard of living for the agricultural community and the objective of ensuring the sustainable development of the Union. Therefore, in specific cases, those initiatives should not be subject to the application of Article 101(1) TFEU.
In periods of serious market imbalances, such collective action may involve specific categories of collective action by private operators to contribute to the stability of the sectors concerned. The Commission should be able to provide Union resources from the agricultural reserve to support such actions. Member States should also be able to allocate additional national resources without delay.
In order for sugar beet growers to benefit from improved contractual clarity and to ensure a harmonised contractual framework taking into account the specificity of the sugar beet sector at the same time, the purchase terms in contracts for the delivery of sugar beet should be aligned with the conditions for the use of written contracts in other agricultural sectors.
Regulation (EU) No 1307/2013 should therefore be amended accordingly.
In order to strengthen the position of farmers in the food supply chain, certain provisions of Regulation (EU) 2021/2115 of the European Parliament and of the Council (9 ) should be amended insofar as the types of intervention existing in certain sectors are concerned. Those amendments aim to facilitate farmers to become members of producer organisations or associations of producer organisations recognised under Regulation (EU) No 1307/2013 or to continue to be members of producer organisations or associations of producer organisations recognised under that Regulation, in view of the positive role of producer organisations and associations of producer organisations in relation to the strengthening of producer marketing power for producer organisations. Moreover, to ensure more efficient and targeted support for producer organisations through strategic plans drawn up by Member States under the common agricultural policy (Strategic CAP Plans), provision should be made for the possibility of increasing Union financial assistance for certain operational programmes in certain sectors.
In some Member States, the value of fruit and vegetable production marketed by producer organisations compared to the total value of fruit and vegetables is still significantly below the Union average. In order to improve competitiveness, Member States can already bring national financial assistance under Article 53 of Regulation (EU) 2021/2115 to producer organisations located in certain regions where the organisation level is significantly below the Union average. In order to strengthen competitiveness, strengthen the positions of farmers in the value chain and set up new producer organisations, financial incentive should be granted to producer organisations in the Member States where the organisation level of producers is below 10 % for 3 consecutive years prior to the implementation of the relevant operational programme, consisting of an increase of 10 % in the financial assistance from the Union.
In order to facilitate the renewal from generation to generation in the farming sector and to encourage new producers to enter producer organisations in the fruit and vegetables and other sectors referred to in Article 42, point (f), of Regulation (EU) 2021/2115, there should be granted a incentive to young farmers and new farmers entering a producer organisation recognised under Regulation (EU) No 1307/2013. In particular, it should be possible to increase Union financial assistance by 20 percentage points for expenditure related to investments on the premises of young farmers or on the premises of new farmers first entered a recognised producer organisation.
In view of the redirection of adverse climatic events, natural disasters, plant diseases and pests in recent years, it is apparent that it is useful for producer organisations and associations of producer organisations to redirection funds, including Union financial assistance within the operational fund, to interventions necessary to address the consequences of these events. It is therefore necessary to provide for the possibility of increasing Union financial assistance laid down in Article 52(1), Article 62, Article 65(1) and Article 68 of Regulation (EU) 2021/2115 from 50 % to 70 % of the actual expenditure incurred, subject to certain conditions.
In order to support the establishment of forms of intervention in the other sectors referred to in Article 42, point (f) of Regulation (EU) 2021/2115, Member States should be allowed, from 1 January 2022, to have additional flexibility in the allocation of funds to those sectors. Therefore, Member States should be able to use up to 6 % of their allocations for direct payments, after assessing the consequences at the level of income support.
Regulation (EU) 2021/15 should therefore be amended accordingly.
In order to ensure that Union resources from the agricultural reserve can be made available to Member States to support collective actions by private operators in periods of serious market imbalances, the possibility of using the agricultural reserve should be extended to support collective actions where the Commission decides that competition rules do not apply to those activities.
Regulation (EU) 2021/2116 of the European Parliament and of the Council ( 10 ) should therefore be amended accordingly.
