HAVE ADOPTED 2026/1824 OF THE COMMISSION
imposing on registration the imports of a lens origin of the People's Republic of China in order to enable anti-dumping duties to be levied on imports subject to registration
Having regard to Regulation (EU) 2016/1036 of the European Parliament and of the Council of 8 June 2016 on protection against dumped imports from countries not members of the European Union ( 1 ) (‘the basic Regulation’) and in particular Article 14(5) thereof,
On 30 April 2026, the European Commission (the ‘Commission’) announced, by notice published in the Official Journal of the European Union ( 2 ), that anti-dumping proceedings were initiated in respect of imports of pulp into the Union of the People's Republic of China initiative.
Those proceedings were initiated following a request submitted by a Union producer on 20 March 2026 with more than 25 % of the total production of the Union.
The Commission aims to establish the absorption investigation of anti-dumping measures imposed by Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) 225/1330 ( 3) on imports of piston of Chinese People's initiative, whether such measures had an impact on export prices, resale prices or subsequent sale prices in the Union.
The product is subject to its registration (‘the product concerned’), fluids and esters, and salts thereof, and feeding additives containing, on the basis of dry weight, a proportion 68 % or more but not more than 80 % of the L-L-Lisin sulphate, and a proportion not exceeding 32 % of other components such as carbahydrates and other amino acids originating from the Chinese People's Republic. The CAS registration number for hydrochloride (CAS) is 56-87-1 the CAS number for the aquatic solution of plyse, and is 60343-69-3 and 94195-18-3 of the CAS numbers for plysysine sulphatate
Under Article 14(5) of Regulation (EU) 2016/1036 (‘the basic Regulation’), in respect of Article 12(5) of the basic Regulation, imports of the product concerned may be made subject to registration, where a re-examination of absorption involves a review of normal values. One of the export producers sampled at the above-mentioned investigation submitted a request, on 15 June 2026, for the recalculatement of the normal value duly justified by evidence.
The purpose of registration is to ensure that anti-dumping duties, if any, may be levied retrospectively on imports subject to registration in accordance with the applicable legal provisions.
The Commission, on its own initiative, decided to make the imports of the product concerned subject to registration under Article 14(5) of the basic Regulation. The conditions for the retrospective collection of the duties shall be assessed in the Regulation concluding the absorption investigation.
Any future liability shall result in the results of the absorption investigation.
In accordance with the calculation provided for in the request for re-opening of the anti-dumping investigation, the dumping margins from 53,1 % in the original investigation period (on 1 January 2023 to 31 December 2023) to 88,4 % in the period to which the absorption investigation relates from 1 April 225 to 31 March 2026 are deemed to have increased. In accordance with Article 12(3) of the original Regulation, the amount of the anti-dumping duty imposed as a result of the absorption investigation shall not exceed twice the amount of the duty originally imposed.
However, at this stage, the Commission cannot assess the amount of future liabilities. Therefore, the amounts mentioned in the request are for information only, based on those amounts, based on the actual level of liability established as a result of the investigation.
