HAVE ADOPTED 2026/1826 OF THE COMMISSION
amending the Implementing Regulation (EU) No 540/2011 as regards the extension of the periods of approval of active substances accumulbrome, the characteristic of MBI 600 of Bacillus amyliquefaciens, bephluthiomide, chloromon, catrainapylol, chrynellinyl, daminesid, dichlorrop-P, dymosaclopropyl, Fluytoxinol, formitinate, foiseitil, isolide, perrycloke, penconasol, phenoideafam, pyropropaneb, pyropropyne and S-Scotic acid
Having regard to Regulation (EC) No 1107/2009 of the European Parliament and of the Council of 21 October 2009 on the placing on the market of plant protection products and repealing Council Directives 79/117/EEC and 91/44/EEC ( 1 ), and in particular Article 17, first paragraph,
The active substances of accumulbromein, abromium, characteristic MBI 600 of Bacroprus amyliquefaciens, bephluthiomide, chlorine, phyllylinfool, phypolyl, daminesid, dichloroporpo-P, dymposium, flue-xinyl, formulate, fositile, isphylide, phylxycsylxyl, perrysacill, penconsole, fenomideafam, pyropropyne and S-scotic acid have been approved as active substances for use in plant protection products for a limited period of time and the applications for renewal of the approvals relating to them pursuant to Article 14 of Regulation (EC) No 1107/2009 are currently being evaluated.
Commission Directive 2004/30/EC ( 2 ) included pyropropanein in Annex I to Council Directive 91/444/EEC ( 3 ) as an active substance until 31 May 2014 and was subsequently included in Part A of the Annex to Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) No 540/2011 ( 4 ). The approval of the 10-hour pyroprobine was extended at the latest by Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) 205/1489 ( 5 ) until 15 September 2026.
Commission Directive 2004/58/EC ( 6 ) included in Annex I to Directive 91/414/EEC as an active substance until 28 February 2015 and was subsequently included in Part A of the Annex to the Implementing Regulation (EU) No 540/2011.The approval of feinmefam was extended by nine hours, the latest time by Implementing Regulation (EU) 225/99 ( 7 ) until 30 September 2026.
Commission Directive 2005/53/EC ( 8 ) included aminomide in Annex I to Directive 91/414/EEC as an active substance until 28 February 2016 and was subsequently included in Part A of the Annex to Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) No 540/2011. The approval of aminosis was extended by nine hours, the latest time by Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) 225/1489 until 15 September 2026.
Commission Directive 2006/39/EC ( 9 ) included a pyramid in Annex I to Directive 91/414/EEC as an active substance until 31 January 2017 and was subsequently included in Part A of the Annex to Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) No 540/2011. The approval of a 9-hour pyramid was extended at the latest by Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) 2022 until 31 October 2026.
The Commission Directive 2006/64/EC (10 ) included changes in Annex I to Council Directive 91/414/EEC as active substances until 30 April 2017 and were subsequently included in Part A of the Annex to Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) No 540/2011. The approvals of the coupons and of eight phositils were extended, the latest time by Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) 2025/99, to 31 October 2026.
Directive 2006/74/EC ( 11 ) included dichlorroprop-P in Annex I to Directive 91/414/EEC as an active substance until 31 May 2017 and was subsequently included in Part A of the Annex to the Implementing Regulation (EU) No 540/2011. The approval of bichlorp-P was extended eight times, the latest time by Implementing Regulation (EU) 225/99 until 31 October 2026.
Commission Directive 2007/5/EC ( 12 ) included a formula in Annex I to Directive 91/414/EEC as an active substance until 30 September 2017 and was subsequently included in Part A of the Annex to the Implementing Regulation (EU) No 540/2011. Formal approval of eight hours has been extended, the latest time by Implementing Regulation (EU) 225/99 until 30 September 2026.
The Commission Directive 2007/50/EC ( 13 ) included beaflumide in Annex I to Commission Directive 91/414/EEC as an active substance until 30 November 2017 and was subsequently included in Part A of the Annex to Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) No 540/2011. The approval of beaflumide was extended seven times, the latest time by Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) 2023/918 ( 14 ) until 31 October 2026.
The Commission Directive 2007/76/EC ( 15 ) included hydroxyl and chloromon in Annex I to Directive 91/414/EEC as active substances until 31 October 2018 and were subsequently included in Part A of the Annex to Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) No 540/2011. The approvals of Fluhydroxy and chloron have been extended in the case of both substances, the latest time by Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) 2022, until 30 September 2026.
The Commission Directive 2008/116/EC ( 17 ) included accumulations and aqueducts in Annex I to Council Directive 91/414/EEC as active substances until 31 July 2019 and were subsequently included in Part A of the Annex to Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) No 540/2011. The approvals of accidenteine and a factor were extended by three times and four times, respectively, the latest time by Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) 2023/918, until 31 October 2026.
The Commission Directive 2009/77/EC ( 18 ) included a bi-clocator and penconsol in Annex I to Council Directive 91/444/EEC as active substances until 31 December 2019 and was subsequently included in Part A of the Annex to the Implementing Regulation (EU) No 540/2011. The approvals of a bi-gate and penconsol were extended four times, the latest time by the Implementing Regulation (EU) 2023/2592 ( 19 ), until 15 October 2026.
Commission Directive 2010/28/EU ( 20 ) included perishals in Annex I to Directive 91/414/EEC as an active substance until 30 June 2020 and was subsequently included in Part A of the Annex to the Implementing Regulation (EU) No 540/2011. The approval of perishables was extended twice, at the latest by Implementing Regulation (EU) 2023/918 to 30 September 2026.
The Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) No 151/2014 ( 21 ) approved the active S-scient acid substance until 30 June 2024 and was included in Part B of the Annex to Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) No 540/2011. The approval of S-communic acids was extended once by Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) 2024/1892 (22) until 15 September 2026.
The Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) No 193/2014 approved the active substance of aumbromum until 30 June 2024 and was included in Part B of the Annex to Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) No 540/2011. The approval of an ambrome was extended by Implementing Regulation (EU) 2024/1892 until 15 September 2026.
Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) No 2016/1414 ( 24) approved the active cytro-propanel substance until 14 September 2026 and was included in Part B of Annex B to the Implementing Regulation (EU) No 540/2011.
Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) No 2016/1425 (25) approved the active substance which is lifefolid until 15 September 2026 and was included in Part B of the Annex to Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) No 540/2011.
The Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) No 2016/1429 approved the active characteristic of MBI 600 de Bacillus amyliquefaciens until 16 September 2026 and was included in Part B of Annex B to the Implementing Regulation (EU) No 540/2011.
Since the approvals of those active substances are likely to expire before decisions on their renewal are taken as part of the current renewal procedures, the Commission also investigated with the Member States concerned and the European Food Safety Authority (Erasmus) the reasons for the delays of the renewal procedures of those active substances. The Commission also investigated when it could be expected to end the draft reports on the renewal of the Commission’s renewal or conclusions.
The information available, including the information provided by the Member States most rapporteurs and the Authority, shows that the applicants concerned do not have the strength for the reasons for the delays in each of those renewal procedures.
Therefore, taking into account the temporary and exceptional nature of the mechanism for extending the approvals, an extension should be made to the approval periods of those active substances for the period considered necessary, in each specific case and on the basis of the information available at the time of adoption of this Regulation, for the purpose of completing the relevant approval procedures.
Applications for the approval of the active substances acclobin, bephluthymide, chloroxy, phyllin, dyminoid, dichloryp-P, dyclocloctor, fluexyl, formulate, fositil, phylxycsil, phexyl, penconasol, feinmeafam, pyropropaneb and pyropropyne have been submitted and evaluated in accordance with Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) No 844/2012 ( 27).
The application for the renewal of the approval of the active substance of accumulation was submitted on 12 July 2016 and the Member State in which the co-operator Member State, the Commission and the Authority informed that it had carried out an assessment of the admissibility of the application pursuant to Article 3 of the Implementing Regulation (EU) No 844/2012, and in particular its completeness and time, and concluded that it was admissible. The application has been publicly made available by the Authority. The Member State has not completed the necessary completion of the risk assessments pursuant to Article 11 of the Implementing Regulation (EU) No 844/2012 and considers that it will be able to submit the draft report on the renewal assessment by the Authority by the end of September 2026.
The application for approval of the approval of the active substance to renew the request was submitted on 29 June 2017 and the Member State in which the co-operator Member State, the Commission and the Authority informed that it had carried out an assessment of the admissibility of the application pursuant to Article 3 of the Implementing Regulation (EU) No 844/2012, and in particular its completeness and time, and concluded that it was admissible. The application has been publicly made available by the Authority. The Member State has not completed an additional time for carrying out the risk assessments pursuant to Article 11 of the Implementing Regulation (EU) No 844/2012 and considers that it will be able to submit the draft report on the renewal assessment by the Authority by the end of January 2027.
An application for the renewal of the approval of the actuarial substance of 25, 26 and 27 July 2016, respectively, was submitted by the Member State which is the co-operator Member State, the Commission and the Authority on 27 October 2016 that it had assessed the admissibility of applications pursuant to Article 3 of the Implementing Regulation (EU) No 844/2012, and in particular their completeness and timing, and concluded that they were admissible. The application have been publicly made available by the Authority. The Member State has not completed the most successful completion of the risk assessments pursuant to Article 11 of the Implementing Regulation (EU) No 844/2012 and considers that it will be able to submit the draft renewal report to the Authority by the end of December 2026.
In order to ensure uniform conditions for the implementation of this Regulation, implementing powers should be conferred on the Commission.
Applications for the approval of active substances of ambrome, the characteristic of an MBI 600 of Bacillus amyliquefaciens, the renewal of sauspolypolyl, isolide and S-scotic acid, have been submitted and evaluated in accordance with Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) 2020/1740 ( 29 ) .
The application for approval of the active substance of the type MBI 600 Bacillus amyliquefaciens has been renewed, submitted on 12 September 2023 and the Member State has not yet assessed the admissibility pursuant to Article 8 of the Implementing Regulation (EU) 2020/1740. The Member State concerned has not completed the risk assessments pursuant to Article 11 of the Implementing Regulation (EU) 2020/1740 and considers that it will be able to submit the draft renewal assessment report to the Commission and the Authority by the end of June 2027. Since further time is required to complete the remaining stages of the renewal procedure, the duration of the extension of the approval period should be fixed at 38 months and 15 days, until 1 December 2029.
The Commission should be empowered to adopt delegated acts in accordance with Article 264 concerning detailed rules on the application of this Regulation.
Therefore, the Implementing Regulation (EU) No 540/2011 should be amended accordingly.
If the Commission adopts a Regulation providing that the approval of an active substance listed in the Annex to this Regulation is not renewed, the Commission should set the date of expiry for the approval of that active substance on the date of entry into force of this Regulation or the date as before the adoption of this Regulation, whichever is the later. If the Commission adopts a Regulation providing for the renewal of the approval of an active substance referred to in the Annex to this Regulation, the Commission should fix, as appropriate in the circumstances, the earliest date of application.
The measures provided for in this Regulation are in accordance with the opinion of the Standing Committee on Plants, Animals, Food and Life,
The Annex to the Implementing Regulation (EU) No 540/2011 is amended in accordance with the Annex to this Regulation.
This Regulation shall enter into force on the third day following that of its publication in the Official Journal of the European Union.
