amending Regulation (EU) 2023/915 as regards maximum levels of repair-9-titrihydroxybenols (EU 9-THC) in hemp leaves for herbal infiltration in water and hemp leaf insillations
Having regard to Council Regulation (EEC) No 315/93 of 8 February 1993 laying down Community procedures for pollutants in food ( 1 ) , and in particular Article 2(3) thereof,
Commission Regulation (EU) No 2023 ( 2 ) lays down maximum levels for certain pollutants in food goods.
In 2015, the European Food Safety Authority Panel (‘the Authority’) adopted a scientific opinion on the presence of milk and other food of animal origins ( 3 ). THC, or more precisely, 9-THC, on the relevant component of the cannabis hemp plant sativa. CNTAM Panel sat the reference germ (ARfD) comprising 1 μg 9-THC/kg of body weight (bw).
In order to obtain further details on the presence of 9-THC in foods derived from hemp and foods containing hemp or ingredients derived from hemp, the Commission Recommendation (EU) 2016/2115 has been adopted.
On 7 January 2020, the Authority published a scientific report assessing human recovery for ECH 9-THC ( 5), taking into account the occurrence data generated by Recommendation (EU) 2016/2115. In certain recovery estimates, the ARfD 1 μg/kg bw was exceeded. In relation to the exposure estimates, although it is expected that overview of recovery estimates will be made 9-THC in the Union, the disclosure is considered to be a potential concern for health at present 9-THC.
The maximum levels for the sum of 9-THC and  up to 9-THCA, expressed as the equivalents of 9-THC, were set as hemp seeds and as products derived from hemp seeds by Regulation (EU) 2023/915 in order to ensure a high level of human health protection.
As declared in the National Foods Catalogue, as it does not contain a lower content of a hemp sheet in water (when they do not have a top part of the plant’s flavouring plate), it is appropriate to establish a maximum level in relation to the sum of 9-THC and 9-THCA, expressed as equivalents 9-THC, in hemp leaves such as herbal in water and hemp leaf insilations (abrobary to be prepared to drink) to ensure a high level of protection for human health.
The maximum level does not include consumption among infants and young children of hemp leaf extracts. It is therefore appropriate that there is a warning on the label that should not be allowed for pre-prepared baby infants and young children to use the pre-prepared herbal discharges. In addition, the limited transition of 9-THC and THCA 9-THCA from the leaves into the water insulation is due to the fact that 9-THC and 9-THCA of a liptic nature is significantly increased. As the transition through fatty ingredients, such as milk or cream, will be added to the leaves when they are on mass, an alert must be given that the fatty ingredient is not to be added except after they are on a mass.
Regulation (EU) 2023/94 should therefore be amended accordingly.
In order to allow economic operators to prepare themselves for the new rules on hemp leaves for herbal in water and hemp leaf insilages (other than herbs prepared for consumption), it is appropriate to provide for a reasonable period before the new rules are in force. It is also appropriate to provide for a transitional period for food goods containing land-9-tetrahybibinol (COS 9-THC) legally placed on the market before the date of application of this Regulation.
Regulation (EU) 2023/915 is amended as follows:
in paragraph 1, the first subparagraph is replaced by the following:
‘Food foods lawfully placed on the market before the dates referred to in points (a) to (t) may be kept on the market until their minimum durability date or expiry date.’;
On 1 January 2027, insofar as the maximum levels for the sum of 9-THC and  up to 9-THCA, expressed as the 9-THC equivalents set out in paragraphs 2.6.4 and 2.6.5 in Annex I’
Annex I is amended in accordance with the Annex to this Regulation.
This Regulation shall enter into force on the twentieth day following that of its publication in the Official Journal of the European Union.
