An article to be used in conjunction with system-insituation that consists of:
three transparent interconnected plastic tubes, each of which is about 5 cm in length and about 1,5 mm in an outer diameter,
simple connector Luer Lock on with rotating nut and protective cap,
two Luer Lock connectors containing an unintegrated valve integrated, and
The three tubes are connected together with the Y-linker. The single tube is fitted with a Luer Lock connector with a rotating nut and a protective cap, which is the lower of the ‘Y’, and the upper head of the ‘Y’ has a slide clamp and a Luer lock connector where the two tubes are fitted with an integrated irrigated valve. The product is about 15 cm in total.
Luer Lock connectors are used to connect the article to tubes and/or other devices (e.g. syringes) in a way that prevents discharges.
The item is designed for use once in health care facilities, home care, out-patient care and medical transport (e.g. in ambulances) in conjunction with highly skilled systems to bring liquids.
For this purpose, the lower shear of the ‘Y’ side of the patient's access to the system is connected by means of the Luer Lock connector. Since there are two tubes, each with the upper head of the ‘Y’ (inflow) Luer lock, some of them can be simultaneously given when the product is used, without the need to contact the patient's side of access of the insulation system or change it. The irreversible valves create a physical barrier (usual system) and prevent microbir corruption. The flow through the tubes can be controlled by the slips clamps attached to the tubes at the top of the ‘Y’.
The item is presented in individual stereoty packaging.
The classification is determined by general rules 1, 3(b) and 6 as regards the interpretation of the Combined Nomenclature, note 8 to Chapter 39 and the wording of CN codes 3917 and 3917 33 00.
This Regulation shall enter into force on the twentieth day following that of its publication in the Official Journal of the European Union.
The article consists of composite goods containing different components, namely (a) interconnected plastic tubes combined with plastic connectors, (b) clamps and (c) valves.
The article incorporates more than the elements of accessories attached to the valves (see also the Explanation Notes of the Harmonised System (NMCC) to heading 8481, of the seventh paragraph). The interconnected plastic tubes which are required by connectors in relation to the use of the article have an essential role to play. Therefore, that component is the giving of their essential characteristics to the article as a whole and is to be classified according to that component. Therefore, the classification under heading 8481 is excluded as a similar valve or appliance.
Since plastic tubes are required with plastic connectors, they shall be deemed to be plastic tubes fitted with fittings. In view of their objective characteristics and characteristics, interconnected plastic tubes that are required by binders comply with the terms of heading 3917 and comply with the requirements of Note 8 to Chapter 39 (see also the NMCC, General to Chapter 39 and to heading 3917).
Therefore, it is to be classified under CN code 3917 33 00 as other tubes, made of plastics, not reinforced or otherwise merged with other materials, fitted with fittings.
( *1 ) The image is here solely to give information.
HAVE ADOPTED 2026/1874 OF THE COMMISSION
( 1 ) OJ L 269, 10.10.2013, p. 1.ELI: http://data.europa.eu/eli/reg2013/952/oj .
A galvanized threading rod of steel other than stainless steel, with a metric thread for a total length of time, without head or upper, and with a tensile strength of less than 800 MPa, on different lengths (between 1 000 mm and 3 000 mm) and on different diameters (between 4 mm and 36 mm).
They are presented without nuts, cheeks or similar elements.
The goods are used to bind, hang or repair a wide range of equipment and components.
The classification is determined by general rules 1 and 6 on the interpretation of the Combined Nomenclature, by Note 1 (d) to Chapter 72 and by the wording of the CN codes 7318 , 7318 15 and 7318 15 42.
Since the article is threaded and is used for the assembly or attachment of goods so that it can be easily dismantled without damage (see also the Information Notes of the Harmonised System (NMCC) to the heading of 7318 (A), first paragraph), it has the objective characteristics of an article falling within subheading 7318 15 (screwback; see also the NMCC to heading 7318 (A), third paragraph).
Therefore, the classification under CN code 7318 19 00 is excluded as other threaded goods.
Therefore, the goods are classified under CN code 7318 15 42 as screws and other unheaded bolts, of steel other than stainless steel, with a tensile strength of less than 800 MPa.
( *1 ) The images contained therein are intended to give information solely on their face.
HAVE ADOPTED 2026/1894 OF THE COMMISSION
providing for emergency financial support for farmers affected by specific problems affecting their economic viability arising from the Middle East crisis
Having regard to Regulation (EU) No 1307/2013 of the European Parliament and of the Council of 17 December 2013 establishing the common organisation of the markets in agricultural products and repealing Regulations (EEC) No 922/72, (EEC) No 234/79, (EC) No 1037/2001 and (EC) No 1234/2007 ( 1 ), and in particular Article 221(1) thereof,
The crisis in the Middle East has been since 28 February 2026 and the interference with trade flows due to the closure of Stolas Hormuz is negatively affecting farmers producing agricultural products from the basis of soil or taking stock in the Union.
The first concern concerns the decline in fertiliser domestic prices in the Union, consisting of 7,3 % of the intermediate inputs of Union farmers in 2024.
Even before the crisis in the Middle East, the supply and price volatility were also under pressure in the world fertiliser markets. The crisis in the Middle East has made this situation worse and increased the production risks of nitrogen and phospharmacological fertilisers, due to the reduced production of natural gas liquefied, which is highly dependent on the production of nitrogen fertilisers. This caused a sudden increase in both inland prices of fertilisers in the world and in the Union and had an immediate impact on fertiliser price in the Union. This sudden increase in fertiliser prices affects the potential and availability of fertilisers required for a crop 2026/27 production cycle.
In May 2026, average nitrogen fertiliser prices increased by 30 % compared to February 2026, and were about 70 % higher than an average of 2024, while phosphatical fertiliser prices were 22 % higher than an average of 2024.
Increased fertiliser costs affect the economic viability of farmers and the risk that they will affect the use of fertilisers and crop production in terms of quantity and quality within the Union. This increase in the price of fertilisers is occurring when farmers are making key decisions under their farming operations 2026/27, such as decisions in terms of the area or type of crop to be placed, proposed curing rates, and related agromatic practices. Increased fertiliser costs are likely to negatively affect farmers' income in 2027, since the quantities produced are lower. This may also negatively affect food availability in the Union in 2027.
The second concern concerns the increase in energy costs on the farm, and in particular fuel costs used by agricultural machinery, which were 8 % of the total intermediate input production costs paid by farmers in the Union in 2024.
The crisis in the Middle East is still underway, and in particular the closure of Stolas Hormuz, has also significantly affected the navigation of energy products and their production. Due to these circumstances, the supply chain was installed for key energy products, in particular oil and liquefied natural gas, obtained from the Gulf production states. As a result, energy product price volatility has increased worldwide, and fuel and dierents among the most affected energy products. This volatility is already being reflected in the Union energy markets, which creates further economic challenges for farmers.
This will result in a substantial increase in the share of fertiliser and energy costs in farm accounts in 2026. Farms whose specialization is the park bar are affected by the major share of fertiliser and fuel costs compared to their overall input costs. However, the increase in fertiliser prices and energy products significantly affects the production process of each farm type.
Meanwhile, there are many prices of agricultural products, especially cereal crops such as cereals, still fixed, which make the ratio of input costs and prices received by farmers for their agricultural products worse.
The increasing input costs due to the crisis still taking place in the Middle East and the way it develops is likely that this will lead to liquidity problems for farmers and endanger their economic viability. Even if the crisis in the Middle East was resolved in the near future, its effects on input prices across the world are likely to continue to exist in the coming months, leading to a continuous impact on farmers.
